Z||000006||ToddAsh||04-11-2001||09:36 AM|||| Yes,I used a stainless steel pipe,It's not too loud just right. I'm 48 years old with a big block 4-speed '56 Chevy that DOES set off the car alarms.For Classic tags in Pa. your car has to be 15 years old(actually 16 years old)the '85s were eligible as of Jan 1,2001. I got mine on Jan. 2nd.Love this car.I'll be looking for a GT or a V8 conversion at Carlisle.Todd Ash||||reg||1
Z||000007||Getaway2k||04-11-2001||07:39 PM||||Hey Standard- I have my 86 GT set up that way. Stainless steel with a cat and 2 resonator tips, no muffler. It sounds great!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||04-11-2001||08:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>  [b]THRUMMMMMMM!!!</B><BR>Been there. Done that. You can't carry on a conversation with the windows down. Kinda reminds me of the pressure wave that occurs when you remove the sunroof, leave the deflector off, and roll up the windows. It's NOT pleasant. I ended up having a bullet glasspack welded in, upstream from the split. It's better, but still loud enough to set off car alarms in the parking garage at work. (Security just <I>loves</I> me.)<BR>Of course, this is a 44 year old's point of view. Your mileage may vary.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds perfect for a 20 year old  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||04-12-2001||06:10 AM||||Had the 2.5 inch - Cat - resonator tips combo and it sounded good but the low frequency resonance just made it sound too much like the muffler had a hole. Reving it was cool, downshifting always brought a smile to my face but when you live on a LONG grade hill the low frequency resonance SUCKS! I even tried a Cadillac Cat that has a muffler built in (looong cat!) and that didn't quiet down the resonance, (or boom as it was called by Raydar) and thats when I finally got my fixit ticket for loud exhaust.<P>So right now I have a long tube glass pack instead of the cat which allowed me to take off the resonator tips. I still have the BOOM and I lost some of the rumble. I will be returning part of the exhaust back to stock so I can put in the Flowmaster. With all of my "experimenting" I will have my choice of straight pipe, cat AND muffler for my exhaust in the stock cat position.||||reg||9
Z||000010||84Bill||04-12-2001||11:26 AM||||Yo Todd!<BR>How did you and where did you get it to pass emissions!? <BR>The engine in my 84 is clunking something awfull, how much do you want for that ole duke you got there? I misplaced your # again or I'd call. I need to know soon so I can get this thing in shape for Carlisle.<P>Thanks||||reg||10
Z||000011||Raydar||04-12-2001||10:32 PM||||Anybody ever use a "chambered" muffler?<BR>The one I'm thinking of has no guts in it whatsoever. Just indentions hammered (pressed?) in it, every foot or so. Late 60's Corvettes were available with these, mounted as sidepipes under a heatshield. Louder'n hell but sounded great.<BR>Guy that lives near me said he bought an 84 Duke a few years ago that had one. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||5
